#use(std/code)
#use(std/include)

#section{Including Files (##include)}::

#code{#use(std/include)} provides #code{#include("file.yamd")} — a
typst-like #strong{eval-time content splice}: the child file is evaluated
when the call runs (during the collect pass) and its content splices into
the call site. It is not a textual paste

#code(lang: "plain")
	Before the include.

	#include("_include-fragment.yamd")

	After the include.

Live:

Before the include

#include("_include-fragment.yamd")

After the include

Semantics of the sub-file:

#list
	#item: #strong{Fresh namespace} — host #code{#(define …)}s and #code{#use}s do not leak in either direction
	#item
		#strong{Forked context} — the child #emph{reads} the includer's context (a file included under a #code{#section}
		sees #code{'header-depth}, so its sections nest one level deeper, as the fragment's section above does), but its
		writes die with the fork
	#item
		#strong{Shared info} — the child's #code{#def}s resolve the parent's #code{#ref}s and vice versa; anchors stay
		unique across the set

Corner cases:

#list
	#item
		Paths are relative to the #emph{including} file; a missing file is an explicit error naming both the written and the
		resolved path
	#item
		Include cycles are explicit errors printing the whole chain; including the same file twice #emph{sequentially} is
		legal (though duplicate #code{#def}s will error as always)
	#item: Errors inside the child report the child's own file and line
	#item
		Output is identical in single- and multi-file builds — included content is simply part of the page it lands on.
		Contrast with #code{#toctree}, whose children are standalone documents
